WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.000
Roucho March!

00:06.000 --> 00:09.000
And you'll bet your life!

00:13.000 --> 00:17.000
Roucho sent me to see the new D. Soto!

00:17.000 --> 00:20.000
Roucho sent me and I love to drive this car!

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
It's long and low and roomier, so handsome you can see!

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
It's powerful and I'm so glad that Roucho sent me!

00:27.000 --> 00:30.000
Listen to him when you hear Groucho say!

00:30.000 --> 00:37.000
So drive the new D. Soto at the Soto Plymouth Dealers today!

00:40.000 --> 00:44.000
Here I am again with two thousand dollars for one of our couples, and many of them say the secret word.

00:44.000 --> 00:48.000
This moth-eating duck will fly down and pay him a hundred dollars.

00:48.000 --> 00:49.000
The word tonight is food.

00:49.000 --> 00:50.000
Swamp.

00:51.000 --> 00:52.000
Abodeci!

00:53.000 --> 00:55.000
George, proceed.

00:55.000 --> 00:58.000
Well, Groucho, we have a couple of young single people for you tonight.

00:58.000 --> 01:01.000
And their names are Miss Barbara Schmidt and Mr. Mario Doré.

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
So would you come in please, folks, and meet Groucho March.

01:06.000 --> 01:09.000
Welcome, welcome to the Soto Plymouth Dealers.

01:09.000 --> 01:11.000
Say the secret word and you'll divide a hundred dollars.

01:11.000 --> 01:14.000
It's a common word, something you'll find around the house.

01:14.000 --> 01:18.000
Barbara Schmidt and Mario Doré, me, eh, eh.

01:18.000 --> 01:20.000
Which one is Barbara?

01:20.000 --> 01:21.000
I am.

01:21.000 --> 01:22.000
You're Barbara.

01:22.000 --> 01:26.000
That's about the silliest question I guess I've ever asked on this show.

01:26.000 --> 01:27.000
How old are you, Barbara?

01:27.000 --> 01:28.000
I'm eighteen.

01:28.000 --> 01:29.000
Eighteen, eh?

01:29.000 --> 01:31.000
A lovely age for a girl.

01:31.000 --> 01:34.000
In fact, it's a lovely age for a woman of forty.

01:36.000 --> 01:40.000
Miss, eh, you're not married, are you, Barbara?

01:40.000 --> 01:41.000
No, I'm not.

01:41.000 --> 01:42.000
You're not, eh? Are you engaged?

01:42.000 --> 01:43.000
No.

01:43.000 --> 01:44.000
Completely free agent?

01:44.000 --> 01:46.000
I'm completely unattached.

01:46.000 --> 01:47.000
Is that so?

01:47.000 --> 01:49.000
You mean your zip is broken?

01:49.000 --> 01:53.000
Well, something's holding you together and I wish it was me.

01:55.000 --> 01:57.000
Where are you from originally, Helen?

01:57.000 --> 01:59.000
Originally I'm from Albany, New York.

01:59.000 --> 02:00.000
Albany, eh?

02:00.000 --> 02:02.000
Yes, and now I live in Pasadena.

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
Well, tell me, do you go to school, do you have a job,

02:05.000 --> 02:08.000
or are you self-sustaining or self-supporting or what?

02:08.000 --> 02:12.000
No, I go to school, I go to UCLA and I'm majoring in English.

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
Oh, that's pretty good.

02:15.000 --> 02:17.000
Do you speak it at all?

02:17.000 --> 02:19.000
Do you speak it at all?

02:21.000 --> 02:23.000
Why did you come to California to learn English?

02:23.000 --> 02:25.000
Don't they speak English in Albany?

02:25.000 --> 02:28.000
Yes, well, I prefer the climate here in California.

02:28.000 --> 02:29.000
Oh.

02:30.000 --> 02:32.000
You're Mario, is that your name?

02:32.000 --> 02:33.000
Mario.

02:33.000 --> 02:34.000
You're not Mario Alonzo, are you?

02:34.000 --> 02:36.000
No, I'm Mario Dure.

02:36.000 --> 02:38.000
Are you related to Mario Alonzo?

02:38.000 --> 02:40.000
No, but Aldo Ray is my brother.

02:41.000 --> 02:43.000
He's related to Mario Alonzo?

02:43.000 --> 02:44.000
No, he's my brother.

02:44.000 --> 02:45.000
Your brother is Aldo Ray?

02:45.000 --> 02:48.000
Well, congratulations, you're very lucky.

02:52.000 --> 02:54.000
Now then, who is Aldo Ray?

02:56.000 --> 02:57.000
He's a movie star.

02:57.000 --> 02:58.000
He's a movie star?

02:58.000 --> 02:59.000
Oh, movie star.

02:59.000 --> 03:02.000
The only movie star I know is Francis X. Bushman.

03:05.000 --> 03:07.000
You're a pretty big brute, Mario, aren't you?

03:07.000 --> 03:08.000
I'm big, yeah.

03:08.000 --> 03:09.000
Yeah.

03:09.000 --> 03:12.000
I'm surprised you don't play football, why is that?

03:12.000 --> 03:15.000
Well, I do play football, I play for the University of Southern California.

03:19.000 --> 03:23.000
You play with USC? That's just what I said, I'm surprised you don't play football.

03:28.000 --> 03:30.000
You hate USC, Bob?

03:30.000 --> 03:31.000
I don't hate it.

03:31.000 --> 03:32.000
No.

03:32.000 --> 03:33.000
I'm for UCLA.

03:33.000 --> 03:34.000
So am I.

03:36.000 --> 03:38.000
Only in the last five minutes.

03:38.000 --> 03:40.000
Up to now, I was a fan of Rutgers.

03:43.000 --> 03:46.000
Now, Barbara, I imagine life must be interesting for a pretty girl in college.

03:46.000 --> 03:50.000
I've never been a pretty girl in college, but I'm only guessing.

03:51.000 --> 03:53.000
I wasn't even a pretty girl in high school.

03:55.000 --> 03:57.000
Was anything exciting ever happen to you, Barbara?

03:57.000 --> 04:03.000
The most exciting thing that ever happened to me was I was chosen the 1954 Rose Queen, Pasadena.

04:03.000 --> 04:05.000
Oh, you were queen of the rose?

04:05.000 --> 04:08.000
Yes.

04:11.000 --> 04:13.000
That's a very high honor, congratulations.

04:13.000 --> 04:14.000
Thank you.

04:14.000 --> 04:16.000
Pretty tough competition.

04:16.000 --> 04:18.000
Yes, it was quite a bit.

04:19.000 --> 04:23.000
Well, enough, let's get down to brass tacks, we've had enough of this historical stuff.

04:23.000 --> 04:25.000
Mario, will you marry this girl?

04:25.000 --> 04:26.000
No, I can't.

04:26.000 --> 04:28.000
You can, you're going steady right now.

04:28.000 --> 04:31.000
Well, call her up and tell her you're going to marry Barbara.

04:31.000 --> 04:34.000
She'll understand, women are very understanding that way.

04:36.000 --> 04:40.000
I'll say, your girl must be quite a dish, Mario, if you'll turn down the Rose Queen for her.

04:40.000 --> 04:42.000
How did you meet your Linna Morata?

04:43.000 --> 04:50.000
Well, I met her about two years ago at a dance and I liked her, so a couple of weeks later I asked her out and that's it.

04:50.000 --> 04:52.000
So, you're going to marry her?

04:52.000 --> 04:53.000
Yes.

04:53.000 --> 04:58.000
I met her at a dance and I liked her, so a couple of weeks later I asked her out and that's it.

04:58.000 --> 05:00.000
We've been going out ever since.

05:00.000 --> 05:03.000
If you were so crazy about her, why did you wait two weeks?

05:03.000 --> 05:05.000
Were you saving up a dime for the phone?

05:06.000 --> 05:08.000
I was busy doing other things.

05:10.000 --> 05:12.000
Other things?

05:12.000 --> 05:17.000
My boy, take it from an old hand on these matters, there are no other things.

05:17.000 --> 05:20.000
Why is this dazzler? Is she out front hitting on you?

05:20.000 --> 05:24.000
No, she's in Pinole, California, that's about 400 miles north of L.A.

05:24.000 --> 05:26.000
She's 400 miles from here?

05:26.000 --> 05:27.000
Yeah.

05:27.000 --> 05:30.000
She's a secretary for the district attorney up there.

05:30.000 --> 05:32.000
Oh, that's bad.

05:35.000 --> 05:41.000
You mean your girl is 400 miles away and you're turning down a date with probably the most beautiful girl in America who is standing right next to you?

05:41.000 --> 05:43.000
I have to, I guess.

05:43.000 --> 05:49.000
No choice. You know, that's like living in Las Vegas and going all the way to Cedar Rapids just to play bingo in a church bazaar.

05:57.000 --> 06:03.000
Well, you're an attractive couple and Mario, if you're smart, you'll marry this girl as soon as she can support you.

06:03.000 --> 06:06.000
I forgot to ask you one question. Do you have a fella?

06:06.000 --> 06:07.000
No.

06:07.000 --> 06:08.000
Why not?

06:08.000 --> 06:09.000
I don't.

06:09.000 --> 06:13.000
I forgot to ask you one question. Do you have a fella? Or did I ask you? Why not?

06:13.000 --> 06:15.000
I have many fellas.

06:15.000 --> 06:18.000
Oh, I see. All right, let's play you a bet you'll win.

06:19.000 --> 06:21.000
I had a fella wanting to meet you.

06:22.000 --> 06:23.000
It was me.

06:25.000 --> 06:28.000
You both know the rules of this swindle? This game?

06:28.000 --> 06:29.000
Yes, sir.

06:29.000 --> 06:33.000
You selected the musical category. These are all top tunes of the last 20 years.

06:33.000 --> 06:36.000
And Fenerman, just keep looking right here.

06:36.000 --> 06:40.000
Okay, now what do you want to start with? 10, 20, all the way to 100.

06:40.000 --> 06:42.000
50, 70, 70.

06:42.000 --> 06:43.000
70?

06:43.000 --> 06:44.000
70.

06:44.000 --> 06:48.000
Okay, this song is from the score of the musical Knickerbocker Holiday. Now you give me the title.

06:56.000 --> 06:57.000
September Song.

06:57.000 --> 06:59.000
September Song is absolutely right.

07:01.000 --> 07:04.000
And you also get a chance to have $170.

07:04.000 --> 07:06.000
Now, what are you going to take a fling at?

07:07.000 --> 07:10.000
70, 70, 70, 80, 80?

07:10.000 --> 07:11.000
We'll go 80.

07:11.000 --> 07:12.000
$80.

07:12.000 --> 07:17.000
Sammy Kahn and Julie Stein wrote this song about 10 years ago. What's the name of it? Play it.

07:28.000 --> 07:29.000
Let It Snow, Let It Snow?

07:29.000 --> 07:34.000
That's right. Let It Snow is right.

07:34.000 --> 07:36.000
Now I have $250.

07:36.000 --> 07:38.000
What are you going to go for now?

07:38.000 --> 07:40.000
90. We'll go for 90.

07:40.000 --> 07:44.000
90. This song was a big hit a few years ago. Let's see if you can identify it.

07:51.000 --> 07:52.000
Wish You Were Here?

07:52.000 --> 07:54.000
Wish You Were Here is right.

07:54.000 --> 07:57.000
Now I have $340.

07:57.000 --> 08:00.000
Last chance to beat the other couples. What are you going to go for?

08:00.000 --> 08:01.000
We'll go $100.

08:01.000 --> 08:07.000
$100. This song was written by Rodgers and Hammerstein. What is the title of it? Play it, Jack.

08:14.000 --> 08:15.000
Hello, Young Lover?

08:15.000 --> 08:17.000
That's right. Now give them a big hand.

08:17.000 --> 08:21.000
And you'll wind up with $120.

08:21.000 --> 08:25.000
There goes that girl and the district attorney and everything else.

08:25.000 --> 08:28.000
Well, thanks and good luck with the soda-piment deal.

08:28.000 --> 08:32.000
Crouch or send me to see the new DeSoto.

08:32.000 --> 08:35.000
Crouch or send us, and we want to drive his car.

08:35.000 --> 08:38.000
Oh, what a thrill you're going to feel when you're behind the wheel.

08:38.000 --> 08:41.000
DeSoto is the new DeSoto.

08:41.000 --> 08:42.000
Beautiful.

08:42.000 --> 08:43.000
Beautiful.

08:43.000 --> 08:44.000
Beautiful.

08:44.000 --> 08:46.000
Now I have $300.

08:46.000 --> 08:50.000
I'll go for $300.

08:50.000 --> 08:52.000
You have $300.

08:52.000 --> 08:54.000
You have $300.

08:54.000 --> 08:56.000
You have $300.

08:56.000 --> 08:57.200
behind the wheel.

08:57.200 --> 09:01.040
DeSoto is the smartest car, smartest of the smart cars.

09:01.040 --> 09:04.360
It's so stylish and now it's not just this.

09:04.360 --> 09:10.240
Let's drive the new DeSoto at DeSoto Plymouth dealers today.

09:10.240 --> 09:15.880
From every angle, from here, from here, or from here,

09:15.880 --> 09:18.040
DeSoto is smart.

09:18.040 --> 09:21.960
DeSoto is the car that makes people stop and look.

09:21.960 --> 09:25.480
The car you'll be proud to have standing in front of your house.

09:25.480 --> 09:29.040
It's smart to own the smartest of the smart cars.

09:29.040 --> 09:32.320
Here is DeSoto's smart double cockpit instrument panel

09:32.320 --> 09:35.560
with a new flight control lever, convenient, but out of your way

09:35.560 --> 09:37.280
