09:50.640 --> 09:55.040
Drive a DeSoto before you decide.

09:55.040 --> 09:59.000
Drive the new DeSoto at DeSoto Plymouth dealers today.

10:04.040 --> 10:06.680
Groucho, we have a man with an unusual occupation for you.

10:06.680 --> 10:08.720
He's Mr. Vern Lucius Cameron.

10:08.720 --> 10:10.080
His partner is a housewife.

10:10.080 --> 10:11.880
She is Mrs. Mariana Ehrlich.

10:11.880 --> 10:14.840
So folks, you come in, please, and meet Groucho Marx.

10:14.840 --> 10:16.080
Welcome to your Bet Your Life.

10:16.080 --> 10:17.880
Say the secret word and divide $100.

10:17.880 --> 10:20.840
It's a common word, something you'll find around the house.

10:20.840 --> 10:25.480
Mrs. Mariana Ehrlich and Mr. Vern Lucius Cameron.

10:25.480 --> 10:28.080
A couple of pretty fancy monikers there.

10:28.080 --> 10:30.600
Mariana, where are you from?

10:30.600 --> 10:32.880
I am originally from Czechoslovakia,

10:32.880 --> 10:36.920
and I came over friends in Portugal to the United States.

10:36.920 --> 10:39.920
You came with a friend from Portugal to the United States?

10:39.920 --> 10:42.640
I came with my best friend's husband.

10:42.640 --> 10:44.120
Your best friend is your husband?

10:44.120 --> 10:44.800
Uh-huh.

10:44.800 --> 10:46.880
Well, that may be true in Czechoslovakia.

10:46.880 --> 10:52.880
Whereabouts behind the Iron Curtain did you come from?

10:52.880 --> 10:54.320
Prague, Czechoslovakia.

10:54.320 --> 10:55.320
Prague, huh?

10:55.320 --> 10:58.040
You were poor but Prague at the time?

10:58.040 --> 11:01.000
Would you give us some idea of your age, Mariana?

11:01.000 --> 11:02.960
I'd rather skip that question.

11:02.960 --> 11:03.680
You'd rather skip it?

11:03.680 --> 11:06.640
Well, skip around here, and then give us your age.

11:06.640 --> 11:11.040
I heard once Lula Fasen said that a girl who tells her age

11:11.040 --> 11:12.560
is liable to tell anything.

11:12.560 --> 11:15.280
Well, I expect to learn many other things out of you

11:15.280 --> 11:17.280
before we go on.

11:17.280 --> 11:19.560
You're Jules Vine, Lucius Cameron.

11:19.560 --> 11:20.880
I was named after Jules Vine.

11:20.880 --> 11:21.880
Is that right?

11:21.880 --> 11:22.880
Yes.

11:22.880 --> 11:24.880
He was named first, and I was named after him.

11:24.880 --> 11:26.880
Yeah, that's a good thing.

11:26.880 --> 11:28.080
Where are you from, Vine?

11:28.080 --> 11:29.880
I was born in Sioux City, Iowa.

11:29.880 --> 11:32.880
Oh, that's where all the lawyers come from, isn't it?

11:32.880 --> 11:32.880
Is it?

11:32.880 --> 11:33.880
I didn't know that.

11:33.880 --> 11:35.880
Well, it's Sioux City, I imagine that's where.

11:35.880 --> 11:36.880
Oh, I see.

11:36.880 --> 11:39.880
I spent three years in Iowa, three years in Kansas,

11:39.880 --> 11:42.880
and then spent most of my boyhood in Oklahoma.

11:42.880 --> 11:44.880
What sort of work do you do?

11:44.880 --> 11:46.880
Well, I'm a hydrologist.

11:46.880 --> 11:48.880
You mean you eat only vegetables?

11:48.880 --> 11:50.880
What's a hydrologist?

11:50.880 --> 11:53.880
A hydrologist is a man who locates, or a woman,

11:53.880 --> 11:57.880
who locates underground liquids, oil, or water.

11:57.880 --> 11:59.880
You mean like a bootlegger?

11:59.880 --> 12:01.880
Yes, if they're underground.

12:01.880 --> 12:03.880
Well, how do you go about finding water?

12:03.880 --> 12:07.880
Well, I have instruments that I've developed over the years.

12:07.880 --> 12:12.880
I have instruments that I've developed over a period of 32 years of locating,

12:12.880 --> 12:14.880
locating wells.

12:14.880 --> 12:16.880
What makes this thing work?

12:16.880 --> 12:21.880
It takes on a charge from the electrical aura around the body,

12:21.880 --> 12:26.880
and this positive charge causes it to become attracted to the negative charge

12:26.880 --> 12:29.880
coming up by reflection from underground water.

12:29.880 --> 12:31.880
Well, you lost me quite some time.

12:31.880 --> 12:33.880
I just...

12:33.880 --> 12:36.880
Have you ever found any wells for people?

12:36.880 --> 12:38.880
Yes, sir. I've located thousands of them.

12:38.880 --> 12:40.880
I don't know how many thousands.

12:40.880 --> 12:43.880
How much do you charge for finding water? A cent a gallon?

12:43.880 --> 12:46.880
Well, the price ranges from $25 to $100.

12:46.880 --> 12:47.880
A hundred dollars?

12:47.880 --> 12:50.880
For a well, or a hundred dollars a day flat rate.

12:50.880 --> 12:52.880
A hundred dollars a day?

12:52.880 --> 12:53.880
Yes, sir.

12:53.880 --> 12:55.880
You must be finding water because you're suddenly soaking somebody.

12:55.880 --> 12:57.880
That's right.

12:57.880 --> 12:59.880
I'd like to go on talking to you two,

12:59.880 --> 13:01.880
but the time has come to play You Bet Your Life.

13:01.880 --> 13:05.880
In the race for the $2,000, the first couple won $440,

13:05.880 --> 13:07.880
and the secret word is food.

13:07.880 --> 13:09.880
I'm sure you're familiar with this game.

13:09.880 --> 13:10.880
I don't have to explain it to you.

13:10.880 --> 13:12.880
This is a spelling quiz.

13:12.880 --> 13:14.880
This is an old-fashioned spelling bee.

13:14.880 --> 13:16.880
You get only one chance at the correct spelling

13:16.880 --> 13:17.880
and only one answer between you.

13:17.880 --> 13:19.880
I want you to spell the word and then pronounce it.

13:19.880 --> 13:20.880
Is that clear?

13:20.880 --> 13:21.880
Spell it and then pronounce it.

13:21.880 --> 13:23.880
All right. What do you start with?

13:23.880 --> 13:25.880
Ten, 20, 50, all the way to 100.

13:25.880 --> 13:27.880
Seventy?

13:27.880 --> 13:28.880
Seventy suits me.

13:28.880 --> 13:30.880
Seventy suits me, too.

13:30.880 --> 13:34.880
All right. Spell the word, Lieutenant, meaning an officer in military service.

13:34.880 --> 13:37.880
L-I-V-U-P-E-N-A-N-T.

13:37.880 --> 13:38.880
All right.

13:38.880 --> 13:40.880
This kid's from Czechoslovakia.

13:40.880 --> 13:42.880
Imagine.

13:45.880 --> 13:46.880
Now I have $170.

13:46.880 --> 13:48.880
What are you going to go for?

13:48.880 --> 13:50.880
80. 80 suits me, okay?

13:50.880 --> 13:54.880
Spell the word, Alumina, meaning a light silver white metal.

13:54.880 --> 13:55.880
Metal.

13:55.880 --> 14:06.880
L-U-M-I-N-A-M-I-N-A-M-U-N-A-L-U-M-I-N-U-N.

14:06.880 --> 14:07.880
That's right.

14:07.880 --> 14:08.880
Right.

14:11.880 --> 14:13.880
You now have $250.

14:13.880 --> 14:14.880
You went to night school, huh?

14:14.880 --> 14:17.880
I did. I went to night school.

14:17.880 --> 14:19.880
And day school, I think.

14:19.880 --> 14:22.880
I know how to spell better than to pronounce.

14:22.880 --> 14:27.880
Well, spell the word, Fictitious, meaning not real, counterfeit, not genuine.

14:27.880 --> 14:33.880
F-I-C-T-I-T-O-U-S.

14:33.880 --> 14:37.880
F-I-C-T-I-T-I-O-U-S.

14:37.880 --> 14:42.880
F-I-C-T-I-T-O-U-S.

14:42.880 --> 14:44.880
All right, now.

14:44.880 --> 14:46.880
Decide one answer between you now.

14:46.880 --> 14:48.880
What are you going to say?

14:48.880 --> 14:50.880
F-I-O-U-S. I'm sure.

14:50.880 --> 14:52.880
Are you sure?

14:52.880 --> 14:54.880
All right, then you spell it.

14:54.880 --> 14:57.880
F-I-C-T-I-T-I-O-U-S. Fictitious.

14:57.880 --> 14:59.880
That's right. That's right.

14:59.880 --> 15:04.880
You now have $320.

15:04.880 --> 15:08.880
Some illiterate in the front row is hollering you are wrong.

15:08.880 --> 15:09.880
That's right.

15:09.880 --> 15:11.880
Now, it's your last chance to beat the other couple.

15:11.880 --> 15:13.880
What are you going to go for?

15:13.880 --> 15:15.880
Hundred. Hundred of the other people.

15:15.880 --> 15:19.880
All right, spell the word Penitentiary, meaning a state or federal prison.

15:19.880 --> 15:28.880
P-E-N-I-T-I-E-N.

15:28.880 --> 15:38.880
No. Penitentiary. P-E-N-I-T-I-E-N.

15:38.880 --> 15:39.880
All right, come on now.

15:39.880 --> 15:41.880
P-E-N-I-T-I-A-R-Y.

15:41.880 --> 15:42.880
That's right.

15:42.880 --> 15:44.880
Now spell it. One of you spell it.

15:44.880 --> 15:47.880
P-E-N-I-T-E-N-T-I-A-R-Y.

15:47.880 --> 15:51.880
That is right.

15:51.880 --> 15:54.880
And you went all the way. You wind up with $440.

15:54.880 --> 15:57.880
Well, thanks and good luck to the DeSoto Plymouth dealers.

15:57.880 --> 16:04.880
Thank you.

16:04.880 --> 16:07.880
We invited some girls who work for an aircraft plant to our factory tonight.

16:07.880 --> 16:11.880
And just before we went on the air, our studio audience selected June French to be on the show.

16:11.880 --> 16:13.880
And her partner is Mr. Albert Hall.

16:13.880 --> 16:16.880
So folks, would you come in please and meet Raucho Mott.

16:16.880 --> 16:18.880
Welcome, welcome to the DeSoto Plymouth dealers.

16:18.880 --> 16:20.880
Say the secret word and divide $100.

16:20.880 --> 16:23.880
It's a common word, something you find around the house.

16:23.880 --> 16:26.880
Mr. Albert Hall and June French.

16:26.880 --> 16:28.880
June, how old are you?

16:28.880 --> 16:29.880
I'm 21.

16:29.880 --> 16:30.880
21.

16:30.880 --> 16:31.880
What's your hometown?

16:31.880 --> 16:32.880
Mineola, Texas.

16:32.880 --> 16:35.880
Mineola, Texas? Is there a town named Mineola?

16:35.880 --> 16:36.880
Yes, sir.

16:36.880 --> 16:37.880
Where does that near?

16:37.880 --> 16:39.880
Oh, it's about 80 miles east of Dallas.

16:39.880 --> 16:42.880
Well, how far is that from Neiman Marcus?

16:42.880 --> 16:44.880
That is Neiman Marcus.

16:44.880 --> 16:45.880
Are you married?

16:45.880 --> 16:46.880
You are?

16:46.880 --> 16:47.880
Yes.

16:47.880 --> 16:48.880
Well, you're pretty young to be married, aren't you?

16:48.880 --> 16:49.880
I've been married six years.

16:49.880 --> 16:51.880
You were married when you were 15?

16:51.880 --> 16:52.880
Yes.

16:52.880 --> 16:54.880
Boy, they catch them early down there, don't they?

16:54.880 --> 16:56.880
No, I caught him early.

16:56.880 --> 17:02.880
Oh. Well, at least you're honest enough to admit it. Most women are not.

17:02.880 --> 17:05.880
Mr. Hall, where are you from?

17:05.880 --> 17:09.880
Originally from Kansas. Born in Kansas.

17:09.880 --> 17:12.880
Wow.

17:12.880 --> 17:19.880
You're getting angry about it.

17:19.880 --> 17:31.880
This guy's trying to hypnotize me.

17:31.880 --> 17:51.880
I'm not going to ask him anymore, to be honest.

17:51.880 --> 17:57.880
Did you grow up on a farm in Kansas?

17:57.880 --> 17:59.880
Did you grow up on a farm back there in Kansas?

17:59.880 --> 18:01.880
Yes, I left when I was 10 years old.

18:01.880 --> 18:03.880
Your name is Albert Hall?

18:03.880 --> 18:04.880
Yes.

18:04.880 --> 18:07.880
Well, that's in London, isn't it, where the musicians play in the concerts?

18:07.880 --> 18:08.880
Oh, yes.

18:08.880 --> 18:11.880
Did you know that? Were you named after that place?

18:11.880 --> 18:25.880
Evidently. I didn't select the name.

18:25.880 --> 18:30.880
Do you think I have such a soft job up there?

18:30.880 --> 18:37.880
It's the last time I come down here without my black jacket.

18:37.880 --> 18:39.880
Where did you go when you left the farm?

18:39.880 --> 18:45.880
Lincoln, Nebraska.

18:45.880 --> 18:46.880
What were you doing there?

18:46.880 --> 18:48.880
Well, I went to school there.

18:48.880 --> 19:09.880
When I quit school, I got a job on the Nebraska State Journal as a printer's desk.

19:09.880 --> 19:18.880
Will you ask him the next question?

19:18.880 --> 19:20.880
You were a printer's desk. Well, why did you get fired?

19:20.880 --> 19:23.880
Maybe you weren't the type, huh?

19:23.880 --> 19:26.880
I didn't get fired.

19:26.880 --> 19:27.880
Al, are you married?

19:27.880 --> 19:29.880
Oh, yes.

19:29.880 --> 19:31.880
How long have you been married, Al?

19:31.880 --> 19:32.880
42 years.

19:32.880 --> 19:33.880
Is your wife out here with you?

19:33.880 --> 19:36.880
Yes, she's in the audience.

19:36.880 --> 19:39.880
What sort of work have you been doing lately?

19:39.880 --> 19:43.880
Well, I came to Seattle and I got a job on the Seattle Times.

19:43.880 --> 19:48.880
Can you imagine if he doesn't win any money here?

19:48.880 --> 19:52.880
I'm leaving long before that.

19:52.880 --> 19:55.880
You say you went to Seattle and you got a job on the paper?

19:55.880 --> 19:58.880
Seattle Times in the composing room.

19:58.880 --> 20:00.880
I see.

20:00.880 --> 20:07.880
And how long were you there?

20:07.880 --> 20:10.880
Three years.

20:10.880 --> 20:16.880
Maybe I can out-frighten him.

20:16.880 --> 20:21.880
Boy, would he fit in all of Dickens' stories, huh?

20:21.880 --> 20:23.880
Well, Jim, what kind of work do you do?

20:23.880 --> 20:24.880
I'm a messenger.

20:24.880 --> 20:26.880
I took a safe on asking you.

20:26.880 --> 20:27.880
You're a messenger?

20:27.880 --> 20:30.880
Well, what do you do as a messenger? Do you deliver messages?

20:30.880 --> 20:37.880
No, I deliver blueprints and supplies and food or anything else the engineers want.

20:37.880 --> 20:46.880
Oh, well done.

20:46.880 --> 20:54.880
You said food, so you and Gargantua each get $50.

20:54.880 --> 20:55.880
All right, all right, Peter Duck.

20:55.880 --> 20:58.880
Now, who do you deliver these things to?

20:58.880 --> 21:00.880
To the engineers.

21:00.880 --> 21:04.880
Well, how are you dressed? Do you wear this kind of an outfit?

21:04.880 --> 21:07.880
Yes, skirts, blouses, sweaters.

21:07.880 --> 21:08.880
You know, better be careful.

21:08.880 --> 21:10.880
You know, I know something about engineers.

21:10.880 --> 21:14.880
They all have plans of their own.

21:14.880 --> 21:28.880
Do these engineers?

21:28.880 --> 21:35.880
Maybe I can charm them.

21:35.880 --> 21:43.880
Mr. Hall, I am reluctant to do this, but let's get back to you.

21:43.880 --> 21:47.880
What are you doing in Hollywood and who are you frightening?

21:47.880 --> 21:50.880
What are you doing here at present, Mr. Hall?

21:50.880 --> 21:54.880
Well, things got tough up in the mountains.

21:54.880 --> 21:56.880
No money.

21:56.880 --> 22:04.880
I came to Hollywood to find out how they make money.

22:04.880 --> 22:08.880
Well, how do they make money?

22:08.880 --> 22:24.880
I walk up and down Hollywood boulevard and I come to the conclusion that 50% of them are on relief.

22:24.880 --> 22:32.880
And then I think the other 40% are going around to these banks and loan companies.

22:32.880 --> 22:34.880
There's three or four in every box.

22:34.880 --> 22:37.880
I think you've got something there.

22:37.880 --> 22:41.880
Have you decided on the type of work that you'd like to do in Hollywood?

22:41.880 --> 22:42.880
What would you like to do?

22:42.880 --> 22:45.880
How long as you're out here now, you're not doing anything.

22:45.880 --> 22:59.880
What you're doing there looks kind of tough.

22:59.880 --> 23:03.880
It is, but I don't want it to get around, that's all.

23:03.880 --> 23:07.880
I guess the jig is up.

23:07.880 --> 23:08.880
Well, I'll change it up for loose chatter.

23:08.880 --> 23:10.880
Now, let's play you bet your life.

23:10.880 --> 23:15.880
In the race for the $2,000, the first two couples are tied with $440.

23:15.880 --> 23:18.880
You both understand the rules of the game.

23:18.880 --> 23:20.880
Now, you select the geography.

23:20.880 --> 23:23.880
And remember, the more the question is worth, the harder it is.

23:23.880 --> 23:25.880
I think 100.

23:25.880 --> 23:27.880
How much?

23:27.880 --> 23:28.880
She says 100.

23:28.880 --> 23:30.880
100, okay.

23:30.880 --> 23:34.880
What country is separated by 1,000 miles of the Republic of India?

23:34.880 --> 23:36.880
Pakistan.

23:36.880 --> 23:40.880
Pakistan is right.

23:40.880 --> 23:41.880
Well, you're all through this match.

23:41.880 --> 23:43.880
You now have $200.

23:43.880 --> 23:48.880
Now, just so that we don't have any confusion on the next questions, consult before you answer.

23:48.880 --> 23:52.880
Because he might have said something else, and you wouldn't have won the money.

23:52.880 --> 23:54.880
All right, what are you going to go for now?

23:54.880 --> 23:55.880
$90.

23:55.880 --> 23:57.880
$90.

23:57.880 --> 23:58.880
Now, one answer.

23:58.880 --> 24:00.880
What great river is sacred to the Hindus?

24:00.880 --> 24:04.880
It empties into the Bay of Bengal.

24:04.880 --> 24:05.880
Ganges.

24:05.880 --> 24:11.880
Ganges is right.

24:11.880 --> 24:13.880
You now have $290.

24:13.880 --> 24:15.880
Hey, you're pretty lucky to have a gal like that.

24:15.880 --> 24:16.880
Always.

24:16.880 --> 24:19.880
I've always been lucky all my life, Groucho.

24:19.880 --> 24:21.880
I'm glad to hear that, Al.

24:21.880 --> 24:22.880
Now, what are you going to go for?

24:22.880 --> 24:23.880
$80.

24:23.880 --> 24:24.880
Yeah.

24:24.880 --> 24:25.880
$80.

24:25.880 --> 24:29.880
The city of Buffalo, New York, is located on which of the Great Lakes?

24:29.880 --> 24:30.880
Erie.

24:30.880 --> 24:32.880
Lake Erie is right.

24:32.880 --> 24:35.880
What happened to that talk I gave you?

24:35.880 --> 24:37.880
You now have $370.

24:37.880 --> 24:40.880
Now, what are you going to go for?

24:40.880 --> 24:43.880
This is your last chance to beat the other couple.

24:43.880 --> 24:44.880
$70.

24:44.880 --> 24:45.880
$70.

24:45.880 --> 24:46.880
What is the largest city in Finland?

24:46.880 --> 24:47.880
It is also the capital.

24:47.880 --> 24:48.880
Now, one answer.

24:48.880 --> 24:52.880
Talk it over, please.

24:52.880 --> 24:56.880
Helsinki.

24:56.880 --> 24:57.880
Helsinki.

24:57.880 --> 24:58.880
That's right.

24:58.880 --> 24:59.880
Helsinki.

24:59.880 --> 25:05.880
And you'll wind up with $440.

25:05.880 --> 25:07.880
And that means that all three of our couples tonight...

25:07.880 --> 25:09.880
How can everybody marry in this show if they're married or not?

25:09.880 --> 25:11.880
It doesn't make any sense.

25:11.880 --> 25:14.880
That means that all three of our couples tonight, in just one minute,

25:14.880 --> 25:17.880
will get the chance of the DeSoto Plymouth $2,000 question.

25:17.880 --> 25:18.880
Everybody, tie.

25:18.880 --> 25:29.880
Now, I have a question for you, and it's a very important one.

25:29.880 --> 25:31.880
Is your car safe to drive?

25:31.880 --> 25:33.880
Can you see safely?

25:33.880 --> 25:35.880
Can you steer safely?

25:35.880 --> 25:37.880
Can you stop safely?

25:37.880 --> 25:39.880
Well, if you're not absolutely sure,

25:39.880 --> 25:42.880
take your car to your DeSoto Plymouth dealer tomorrow.

25:42.880 --> 25:45.880
You make certain your car is a safe car.

25:45.880 --> 25:51.880
You'll make an expert check of your brakes, tires, headlights, tail lights,

25:51.880 --> 25:54.880
steering, and all other important safety features.

25:54.880 --> 25:57.880
He'll make sure your car is safe and dependable

25:57.880 --> 26:00.880
and tell you if you need any adjustments or repairs.

26:00.880 --> 26:04.880
And if you do, you'll make them quickly and at a reasonable cost.

26:04.880 --> 26:07.880
His technicians are specially trained,

26:07.880 --> 26:11.880
and they use the very latest equipment and factory-approved methods.

26:11.880 --> 26:13.880
They'll make your car a safe car.

26:13.880 --> 26:16.880
From headlights that enable you to see clearly at night

26:16.880 --> 26:20.880
to tail lights that enable other drivers to see you.

26:20.880 --> 26:24.880
Everything that's important to your safety will be put in tip-top condition,

26:24.880 --> 26:26.880
and it won't take long.

26:26.880 --> 26:31.880
In a short time, your DeSoto Plymouth dealer will make your car a safe car

26:31.880 --> 26:33.880
and at a reasonable cost.

26:33.880 --> 26:35.880
No matter what make of car you drive,

26:35.880 --> 26:38.880
visit your DeSoto Plymouth dealer tomorrow.

26:38.880 --> 26:42.880
Make sure your car is a safe car.

26:42.880 --> 26:47.880
Well, here are the three couples all tied for the DeSoto Plymouth $2,000 question.

26:47.880 --> 26:48.880
We've given them little slips of paper.

26:48.880 --> 26:50.880
They'll write down one answer between them,

26:50.880 --> 26:54.880
and if they all get it right, we'll split the money among all of them.

26:54.880 --> 26:58.880
For $2,000, what was the name of the famous English jurist

26:58.880 --> 27:02.880
whose commentaries are fundamental in any study of English law?

27:02.880 --> 27:20.880
♪

27:20.880 --> 27:21.880
All right, what are the answers?

27:21.880 --> 27:26.880
Mr. Dore?

27:26.880 --> 27:30.880
Robert Schmidt and Mario Dore's answer is nothing.

27:30.880 --> 27:34.880
June French and Al Hall's answer is nothing.

27:34.880 --> 27:37.880
Mariana Ehrlich and Brian Cameron's answer is also nothing.

27:37.880 --> 27:39.880
This one has got oil, but that's wrong.

27:39.880 --> 27:44.880
It's Sir William Blackstone, a very famous name in the history of jurisprudence.

27:44.880 --> 27:45.880
I'm sorry you all lost.

27:45.880 --> 27:49.880
That means the big question next week will be worth $2,500.

27:49.880 --> 27:53.880
Well, they lost the big money, but they all did pretty well on the quiz, didn't they, George?

27:53.880 --> 27:54.880
Yes, all the way.

27:54.880 --> 27:55.880
How much did they each win?

27:55.880 --> 27:57.880
Each couple won $440.

27:57.880 --> 28:00.880
Congratulations to all of you and to all of our contestants on the show tonight

28:00.880 --> 28:02.880
and to both of you and to everybody else.

28:02.880 --> 28:10.880
♪

28:10.880 --> 28:13.880
Friends, go in to see your DeSoto Plymouth dealer tomorrow,

28:13.880 --> 28:16.880
and when you do, tell them Groucho sent you.

28:16.880 --> 28:21.880
♪

28:21.880 --> 28:24.880
Be sure to tune in next week, same time, same station,

28:24.880 --> 28:27.880
to watch your marks in You Bet Your Life on television,

28:27.880 --> 28:29.880
on radio every Wednesday night,

28:29.880 --> 28:34.880
and don't miss the big Chrysler Corporation TV show each week on another network.

28:34.880 --> 29:03.880
♪

29:03.880 --> 29:07.880
This is George Benneman signing off with a message from the National Safety Council.

29:07.880 --> 29:12.880
The month of May has been designated as National Car Safety Check Month.

29:12.880 --> 29:16.880
To check accidents, take your car in for a safety check without delay.

